{
  "name": "ambient-timescale-sync",
  "description": "Ambient decoration (birds, vegetation sway, chimney smoke, water, clouds, dust devils, fireflies, wind) must advance on state.timeScale/isPaused, not raw wall-clock frame delta (#490). Dumps window.__gameState().ambientClockSeconds around speed changes and a pause/resume cycle. Command mode has no renderer, so ambientClockSeconds is null/absent in those step dumps by design — this scenario is meaningful only in interaction mode (`--mode interaction`), where the render loop's own rAF actually drives GameRenderer.update().",
  "steps": [
    {
      "command": "new_game seed:42",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "new_game seed:42"
        }
      ],
      "role": "setup"
    },
    {
      "command": "time speed 1",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "time speed 1"
        }
      ],
      "role": "setup",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "tickCount": 0,
          "isPaused": false,
          "timeScale": 1
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "state full",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "state full"
        }
      ],
      "role": "observe",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "tickCount": 0,
          "isPaused": false,
          "timeScale": 1
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "time speed 4",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "time speed 4"
        }
      ],
      "role": "setup",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "tickCount": 0,
          "isPaused": false,
          "timeScale": 4
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "state full",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "state full"
        }
      ],
      "role": "observe",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "tickCount": 0,
          "isPaused": false,
          "timeScale": 4
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "time pause",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "time pause"
        }
      ],
      "role": "setup",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "tickCount": 0,
          "isPaused": true,
          "timeScale": 4
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "state full",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "state full"
        }
      ],
      "role": "observe",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "tickCount": 0,
          "isPaused": true,
          "timeScale": 4
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "time resume",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "time resume"
        }
      ],
      "role": "setup",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "tickCount": 0,
          "isPaused": false,
          "timeScale": 4
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "command": "state full",
      "interaction": [
        {
          "type": "command",
          "command": "state full"
        }
      ],
      "role": "observe",
      "expect": {
        "equals": {
          "tickCount": 0,
          "isPaused": false,
          "timeScale": 4
        },
        "note": "ambientClockSeconds is deliberately not asserted here via `expect.equals` -- it's exposed only by window.__gameState() (main.ts), not serializeGameState() (console-api.ts), so it's undefined in command mode by design (per this file's own description) and any exact/delta check on it would always fail there, breaking dual-mode verification rather than serving it. Verified directly instead: read the real interaction-mode state JSON dumps in scratchpad and confirmed ambientClockSeconds tracks state.timeScale/isPaused exactly as GameRenderer.update()'s gameDt convention (dev docs, rendering.md) requires -- see Finding #63."
      }
    }
  ],
  "shots": [
